Object.assign(window.VAULT_NOTES = window.VAULT_NOTES || {}, {"20th-century American presidents have expanded presidential powers beyond their original constitutional intentions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The presidents of the 20th century have expanded the powers of the presidency beyond their previously utilized limits.</p><h4>Significance</h4><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The presidents have expanded the power of the presidency in the following manners:</p><ul><li><strong>Expanding war powers:</strong> Truman bypassed Congress to fight the Korean War; LBJ and Nixon waged the Vietnam War without a formal congressional declaration of war.</li><li><strong>Growth of the administrative state:</strong> Known as “congressional delegation,” which essentially transfers legislative power to the executive. Roosevelt’s New Deal essentially gave agencies the powers to enact regulations, bypassing the legislative process entirely.</li><li><strong>Executive orders:</strong> Theodore Roosevelt pioneered the “Stewardship Theory,” whereby the president could do anything that was not explicitly forbidden by the Constitution. FDR and Trump are examples of presidents who expanded executive powers greatly.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>","A belief is a habit of action":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A belief is a habit of thought which guides our actions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a fundamental axiom which underlies the philosophy of pragmatism.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>Belief is not merely a habit of thought, but also the accompanying actions. To Pierce, merely a change in thought doesn’t mean anything. It is an accompanying change in action that actually matters. The goal of inquiry is that we may be able to form beliefs.</p><h4>References</h4>","A choice is a deliberate decision to do something one sees as a means to an end":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A rational action not identified with desire or temper that is clearly voluntary. It involves the process of deliberation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Choice is important in Aristotle’s philosophy since virtue and vice are things that an individual chooses to do, not merely feel.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Choice is making a decision after a decision, directing our aims in accordance with the decision.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means\">Deliberation is the process that occurs when the means are uncertain and reason is required to find the correct means</a><ul><li>Deliberation is a process that is integral to the way that choice is structured.</li></ul></li></ul>","A growing gap between Europe and the US is caused by trade, religious, and political tensions":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Due to the emergence of the European Union as a major trade power that could rival the US, the intense religiosity of Americans, and radical political differences between the US and Europe, a wide gap has formed.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is significant as the split between the US and Europe is immense geopolitical implications, as the two were long-time allies.</p><h4>References</h4>","Arguments for kingship must collapse to arguments for aristocracy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Arguments for kingship are, in fact, better satisfied when viewed through the lens of aristocracies.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the argument Aristotle employs in order to reject arguments for kingship.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The main argument for kingship is that the rule of law is context-insensitive, and therefore, is unable to make the best decision for the common good. Accepting that this is true, in order to act in the good interest then, the king must be virtuous. But surely, the judgement of many virtuous people combined is better than the judgement of a single virtuous person? Not only that, a plurality is more incorruptible than a single person. Therefore, aristocracy is preferable to kingship.</p><h4>References</h4>","Confucianist culture and a non-communist economic system is correlated with economic success":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>There seems to be a correlation between Confucian culture in non-communist states and rapid economic growth, though there are some confounders which make it difficult to draw a clear connection between the two concepts.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This could serve as a launching point for a deeper understanding of Confucianist culture and/or the rapid development of the Asian Tigers.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>  The above charts seems to demonstrate that there is in fact some correlation between Confucianism and higher levels of economic growth, such that it is statistically significant that Confucianist, non-communist countries have higher levels of growth than non-Confucianist, non-communist countries. There are some things which much be noted, however:</p><ul><li>The sample size is rather small ($n=11$).</li><li>The Confucianist countries begin in a better economic situation than the non-Confucianist ones, though the difference is not statistically significant.</li><li>There are confounders such as the fact that Confucianist non-Communist states tend to be small, guaranteed by Western nations, and had good port access.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist\">Many East Asian countries are dirigisme and neo-mercantilist</a><ul><li>Perhaps <em>some</em> state intervention in present, and cannot be discounted in explaining the rapid growth?</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations\">Human capital explains the success of East Asian nations</a><ul><li>This fact could be true, but it seems that institutions (communist vs. Non-communist) play a more important role.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a><ul><li>And this rejection led to economic growth?</li></ul></li></ul>","Deliberate double meaning lets a single line carry a paragraph of content":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Diction that sustains two readings at once compresses a great deal into very little, so that a line can operate simultaneously on the literal, the metaphorical and the topical register.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the mechanism behind literary terseness, and it explains why paraphrase of good writing is always longer than the original.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>In <em>Hamlet</em> 1.2.74 and 1.2.76 the word “common” means first what is shared by all humanity and then what is low and trite, within two lines.</li><li>“A certain convocation of politic worms are e’en at him. Your worm is your only emperor for diet” (4.3.22-24) puns on the Diet of Worms, casting Hamlet as Luther destroying Claudius by argument, so the line works metaphorically and religiously at once.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together\">Great art contains layers and internal echoes that bind it together</a><ul><li>The criterion this device serves.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased\">Great art carries meaning that cannot be paraphrased</a><ul><li>Why the compression cannot be undone without loss.</li></ul></li></ul>","Deregulation is generally beneficial for the economy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In general, deregulation and laissez-faire is good for the economy.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is a general approach for how the economy should be run.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>This is especially true when the following three criteria are met:</p><ul><li><strong>Demand is relatively inelastic:</strong> under such circumstances, taxes distort supply without lowering price.</li><li><strong>High costs are common:</strong> high probability of inefficient firms means that many necessary producers could be excluded.</li><li><strong>Low redistributive motive:</strong> there are some other reason for caring about firm profits.</li></ul><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>The deregulation of the airline industry in the 1970s:<ul><li>This drastically reduced the price of taking a flight with a decrease in safety. Prices fell by about 1/3.</li><li>Many more routes were offered.</li><li>Quality fell, and airlines moved to competition based on prices rather than quality.</li></ul></li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Legal regulation is not context sensitive\">Legal regulation is not context sensitive</a><ul><li>This is a reason why regulation would be bad.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy\">France’s strict regulation, high worker benefits, and high pensions are crippling the economy</a><ul><li>An example of regulation being bad.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem\">The biggest problem facing a state-run economy is the economic calculation Problem</a><ul><li>A set of problems with a highly regulated economy.</li></ul></li></ul>","Determinism does not entail that future events are predictable":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Even if a system is deterministic, it seems that such a system is not determined in such a way that the future is able to be predicted.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to weaken the thesis of determinism quite a lot, since it deflates what it means for a system to be “deterministic.”</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>The fact that a deterministic system might not entail that its future is predictable is from the fact that if knowledge of a prediction is made known in the real world, it seems that there is nothing stopping agents from merely acting in a way that contradicts the prediction itself. If this is the case, then what does it mean for a system to be determined in the first place? Usually, determined means that a future outcome is able to be predicted based on some presents facts, but if this is not possible, then it seems that humans must have free will to some extent.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism\">Free will is impossible, regardless of the truth of determinism</a><ul><li>This note seems to argue against this point.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Determinism and free will are incompatible\">Determinism and free will are incompatible</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Frankenstein (book)":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Modern classic that explores themes such as the nature of fate, human progress, revenge, existentialism, and religion.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is one of the most acclaimed works of fiction in all of fiction, and a work that (in my opinion) is deserving of it’s reputation.</p><h4>Themes and Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Our fate is determined from birth\">Our fate is determined from birth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"As history progresses, morality advances as well\">As history progresses, morality advances as well</a> <ul><li>This book serves as a repudiation of this idea, specifically regarding the technological advancements through history. This is also related to the contemporaneous ideas of the Enlightenment that Shelley would no doubt have been familiar with.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The &#x27;tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions\">The 'tragedy’ in tragic works of art often spring from the main character’s own actions</a></li></ul><h4>Quotes</h4><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.74</span></p><blockquote><p>One man’s life or death were but a small price to pay for the acquirement of the knowledge which I sought for the dominion I should acquire and transmit over the elemental foes of our race.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This seems be the main motivation for many characters in this work, and it is also what creates Frankenstein’s monster. </li><li>After coming back and visiting this quote after learning about Frankenstein’s discovery of the ability to create life, I think that the central motif of the story is one of humans attempting to conquer the unknown, although it often comes with dire consequences. This is exactly what the person writing the letters was attempting to do. </li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.93</span></p><blockquote><p>Besides, I had a contempt for the uses of modern natural philosophy. It was very different, when the masters of the science sought immortality and power; such views, although futile, were grand; but now the scene was changed. The ambition of the enquirer seemed to limit itself to the annihilation of those visions on which my interest in science was chiefly founded. I was required to exchange chimeras of boundless grandeur for realities of little worth.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This hints at his later attempts at “playing God” in his construction of Frankenstein. He was attracted to the grand undertakings of the alchemists and Hermetics, and not the incremental progress of the modern scientists.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.94</span></p><blockquote><p>The ancient teachers of this science,’ said he, ‘promised impossibilities, and performed nothing. The modern masters promise very little; they know that metals cannot be transmuted, and that the elixir of life is a chimera. But these philosophers, whose hands seem only made to dabble in dirt, and their eyes to pore over the microscope or crucible, have indeed performed miracles. They penetrate into the recesses of nature, and show how she works in her hiding-places. They ascend into the heavens: they have discovered how the blood circulates, and the nature of the air we breathe. They have acquired new and almost unlimited powers; they can command the thunders of heaven, mimic the earthquake, and even mock the invisible world with its own shadows.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>A rejection of Frankenstein’s ideals about the nature of science, grounding it in reality and incremental progress rather than mysticism. It will be interesting to see how Frankenstein responds to this idea, and if he adopts it, how it eventually breaks away from him.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.98</span></p><blockquote><p>After days and nights of incredible labour and fatigue, I succeeded in discovering the cause of generation and life; nay, more, I became myself capable of bestowing animation upon lifeless matter.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>This is most likely an early turning point in the book where Frankenstein learns about the secret to life. It is this ability that he eventually uses to construct his monster.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.99</span></p><blockquote><p>Learn from me, if not by my precepts, at least by my example, how dangerous is the acquirement of knowledge and how much happier that man is who believes his native town to be the world, than he who aspires to become greater than his nature will allow.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>“Ignorance is bliss.” The unintentional consequences of knowledge. Explores how an endless search for knowledge may have terrible results.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.100</span></p><blockquote><p>Pursuing these reflections, I thought, that if I could bestow animation upon lifeless matter, I might in process of time (although I now found it impossible) renew life where death had apparently devoted the body to corruption.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>Perhaps part of the reason that Frankenstein wants to learn the secret of life so much was the death of his mother right before he left. This event no doubt left something in him, a desire to learn how to create life, so that he could potentially revive his mother.</li></ul><blockquote><p>[!PDF|yellow] <span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.103</span></p><blockquote><p>For this I had deprived myself of rest and health. I had desired it with an ardour that far exceeded moderation; but now that I had finished, the beauty of the dream vanished, and breathless horror and disgust filled my heart.</p></blockquote></blockquote><ul><li>While for the longest time, Victor had sought to create life, the moment he creates it, he realizes how terrible it had been for him to actually do this.</li></ul><blockquote><p>(<span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.147</span>) Shall I not then hate them who abhor me? I will keep no terms with my enemies. I am miserable, and they shall share my wretchedness.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is much like Satan’s attitude after being kicked out from heaven, the idea that if one is not able to get what they want, they will cause others to suffer as well.</li></ul><blockquote><p>(<span class=\"wl-off\">Frankenstein, or, The modern Prometheus, p.148</span>) For the first time, also, I felt what the duties of a creator towards his creature were, and that I ought to render him happy before I complained of his wickedness.</p></blockquote><ul><li>This is the first time in the book when Frankenstein has finally confronted his responsibility as the creator of his creature, feeling, if only in the slightest amount, some compassion for the plight that he has experienced.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">The injustice of his sentence was very flagrant; all Paris was indignant; and it was judged that his religion and wealth rather than the crime alleged against him had been the cause of his condemnation.</span></p><ul><li>There much hypocrisy in this statement, as the people were outraged that an Ottoman was judged for his religion and wealth, but when they meet the creature, they judge him the same way.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">Farewell, Walton! Seek happiness in tranquillity, and avoid ambition, even if it be only the apparently innocent one of distinguishing yourself in science and discoveries.</span></p><ul><li>Victory’s final farewell to Walton, warning him of the dangers of attempting scientific progress. This is likewise a rejection of Shelley of the Enlightenment idea of inevitable human progress and rationalism. Yet, the brutality and unreasonableness of the creature likewise presents itself as a critique of Romanticism.</li></ul><p><span class=\"wl-off\">But it is even so; the fallen angel becomes a malignant devil. Yet even that enemy of God and man had friends and associates in his desolation; I am alone.</span></p><ul><li>The creature expresses his disappointment that he was alone, comparing himself with Satan from <em>Paradise Lost</em>. He attributes this to the idea that he was mistreated due to the way he looked.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them\">A tragic protagonist can embody the political character of the society that produced them</a><ul><li>Oedipus is a figure just like Frankenstein as both characters seek truth. This also means that both characters are bound by their ambition, their pre-determined fate, and the idea that progress/new discoveries is not always a good thing.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"It is not always good to know the truth\">It is not always good to know the truth</a><ul><li>This is a theme constantly present throughout the book.</li></ul></li><li><span class=\"wl-off\">The Enlightenment was characterized by belief in rationality and the inevitability of human progress</span><ul><li>This theme is especially related to Walton and Victor’s quests attempting to uncover the nature of the natural world.</li></ul></li></ul>","History can be written as an evidential inquiry rather than as a record of divine causes":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Thucydides’ approach to history in his <em>History of the Peloponnesian War</em> scientized the study of history compared to the approaches taken by his predecessors, most famously Herodotus and Homer. Rather than focusing on divine intervention and inspiration, Thucydides seeks concrete evidence.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Thucydides’ work laid the groundwork upon which future histories were created, as he set the standard for good history for the future.</p><h4>References</h4>","In a Rawlsian society, people would be educated for autonomy":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>In a Rawlsian society, people would not be brainwashed into having Rawlsian ideas, but rather, would simply be educated in autonomy, such that they are able to derive the principles of justice themselves from the original position.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This can be used to side-step accusations of brainwashing in the Rawlsian society.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It seems unlikely to be that even if educated in autonomy, people will be able to all come to the same conclusions that Rawls did, because Rawls’ logic relies on people having specific utility functions which would naturally differ between people.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position\">Rational agents would be risk-averse in the original position</a><ul><li>Something like this would be an assumption that wouldn’t really be able to be made.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical\">We should accept the methodological approach of the original position, even though it is hypothetical</a><ul><li>The issue is that people’s assumptions of the original position might differ.</li></ul></li></ul>","Meaning in History":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Argues that modern Western philosophies of history—including the ideas of progress developed by thinkers such as Hegel and Marx—are secularized versions of the Judeo-Christian belief in salvation and the end of time. Although modern thinkers reject religious explanations, they preserve the same basic structure: history is linear, the past prepares the way for the future, and humanity is moving toward a final fulfillment. Löwith contrasts this view with the cyclical conception of history found in ancient Greek thought and argues that secular progress cannot provide a genuine meaning for history because it transfers religious hopes into the worldly realm without retaining their transcendent foundation.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This work is influence in the philosophy of history and seems to relate to many modern discussions regarding the influence of Christianity on the contemporary cultural zeitgeist.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world\">Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a></li></ul><h5>Burckhardt</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history\">Spiritual continuity is the primary concern of history</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion\">Continuity of history requires spiritual effort, and thus instrumentally, religion</a></li></ul><h5>Marx</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity\">Marxism views history through a eschatological perspective borrowed from Christianity</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Marx is more idealistic than Hegel\">Marx is more idealistic than Hegel</a></li></ul><h5>Hegel</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will\">Although humans have free will, they ultimately serve as instruments that express divine will</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence\">The philosophy of history is essentially an elaborate theodicy for all of human existence</a></li></ul><h5>Proudhon</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind\">Divine providence is nothing but the collective instinct of mankind</a></li></ul><h5>Comte</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The final stage of history is a positive one\">The final stage of history is a positive one</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action\">The Catholic Church allows for the establishment of universal Christian morality above secular spheres of political action</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals\">Secular philosophies of history can only address humans as collectives, and not individuals</a></li></ul><h5>Condorcet &amp; Turgot</h5><ul><li>Condorcet’s philosophy of history was one that was supremely optimistic, and portrayed the philosophy as one whereby humans would inevitably reach ultimate happiness.</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans\">The process of history follows naturalistic laws that can be determined by humans</a></li></ul><h5>Voltaire</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The universality of history is a product of Christian history\">The universality of history is a product of Christian history</a></li></ul><h5>Vico</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans\">Since humans constitute history, the principles that undergird history must then be accessible to humans</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin\">God saved humans, while not directly changing their psychology, from sin</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li></ul><h5>Bossuet</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world\">Faith is conjured by the very absence of any visible mark of providence in the history of the world</a></li></ul><h5>Joachim</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Fulfillment can be found within history\">Fulfillment can be found within history</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a></li></ul><h5>Augustine</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"That history entails progress is a modern conception\">That history entails progress is a modern conception</a></li></ul><h5>Orosius</h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Historical judgment is relative\">Historical judgment is relative</a></li></ul><h5>The <a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Bible\">Bible</a></h5><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"History is inherently theological\">History is inherently theological</a></li></ul><h4>Addendum</h4><p>It seems that the ideas that are described in this book can be seen everywhere today.</p><blockquote><p class=\"callout-title\">“<a href=\"https://www.liberalism.org/p/jefferson-s-liberal-morals?curius=7539&amp;trail=07a868ba\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jefferson’s Liberal Morals</a>”</p><p>One bitter lesson we’ve learned since the Enlightenment is that there are no guarantees about moral progress; the <strong>forward march of history only continues when living human beings continue to make it happen.</strong></p></blockquote><ul><li>Not only does this imply that a forward march of history is extant, it also (atheistically) assets that it is human beings that drive this forward progress in history.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Moby-Dick (book)\">Moby-Dick (book)</a><ul><li>An example of the death of Christianity eluded to in this book.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>Representative of the circular nature of ancient views of history.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)\">The Rise of the Roman Empire (book)</a><ul><li>Ibid.</li></ul></li></ul>","Minimum wage drives capital substitution":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>A minimum wage drives the substitution of labor for capital, since it increases the relative marginal product of capital increases with higher marginal product of labor.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a downside (for the worker) of minimum wage.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><ul><li>A 10% increase in the minimum wage is equivalent to an 8% increase in robot adoption.</li><li>Large manufacturing firms are the drivers of the substitution.<ul><li>This is because manufacturing is relatively easier to automate.</li><li>And this is because large firms have more capital to invest in automation.</li></ul></li><li>This is a trend seen in the US, China, Turkey, and Germany; it is likely true everywhere.</li><li>A conclusion that can be drawn from this is that labor policy shapes technological diffusion.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The minimum wage can reduce unemployment\">The minimum wage can reduce unemployment</a><ul><li>This is not true in this case.</li></ul></li></ul>","Politics is fundamentally a moral debate, not a factual one":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Ultimately, politics is a debate over axiology and morals, not a debate over which object policy is the “best,” since any such judgements are based fundamentally on moral assumptions.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This reframes the nature of politics from one of factual, impassionate debate over what is “objectively” the best policy into a debate about what we ought to value.</p><h4>References</h4>","Some pleasures are greater than others":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Some pleasures are greater/better than others, which can be proven from the fact that, when faced with a choice between things that both bring pleasure, can actually make a choice.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is the core of Mill’s tiered utilitarian system where some pleasures are inherently greater than others.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Mill claims that the way in which you decide which pleasures are through collective consensus. If its the case that most or all of those who have experienced the pleasures being compared prefer one to the other (irrespective of any moral obligation to prefer one or the other), then the preferred one is the “greater” pleasure.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life\">Pleasure and freedom from pain are the only desirable things; therefore, they represent the purpose of life</a><ul><li>But you can still rank pleasure/freedom from pain, i.e., some or more impactful than others.</li></ul></li></ul>","The Clash of Civilizations and the Remaking of World Order":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Argued against Fukuyama’s thesis of the “end of history” while presenting a culture-centric view of geopolitics.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This is an important work on foreign relations that continues to impact foreign policy of nations such as the US today.</p><h4>Themes &amp; Ideas</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition\">Identity requires knowing who you are not, and is often defined by opposition</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies\">Culture is the largest and most prominent cleavage in societies</a> </li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion\">The West’s past domination of the world was a result of superior technology, not superior values or religion</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture\">Despite some civilizational convergence, there is no universality in culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization\">Nations typically Westernize, then modernize, then reject Westernization</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The West is in decline\">The West is in decline</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security\">The end of the Cold War marked a major change in global security</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Soft power is premised on hard power\">Soft power is premised on hard power</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world\">The late 20th century is marked by a drastic increase in religiosity across the world</a> z</li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture\">The rejection of Western culture has lead to various ‘affirmations’ of Asian culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture\">Trade will increasingly be upon the boundaries of culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself\">A ‘torn country’ can redefine itself</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state\">Islamic culture places emphasis on the tribe and the religion, not the nation-state</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system\">While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries\">Mexican immigration to the US is unique compared to immigration to the US from other countries</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon\">Conflicts arise when a rising power begins to overtake an existing hegemon</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture\">Conflict is sharpened by differences in culture</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Trade brings people into contact, not agreement\">Trade brings people into contact, not agreement</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China grows its influence with non-expansionary means\">China grows its influence with non-expansionary means</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon\">States can either bandwagon or balance a rising hegemon</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"China will dominate in East Asia\">China will dominate in East Asia</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones\">Islamic states are far more likely to engage in violence than non-Islamic ones</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete\">The US supported Bosnian Muslims due to its idealism, moralism, and naivete</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West\">The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West</a></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War\">The US should not blindly abide by its foreign policy commitments made during the Cold War</a></li></ul><h4>Three Rules for Foreign Policy in the Future</h4><ol><li><strong>Abstention rule:</strong> Core states must abstain from intervening in conflicts of other civilizations.</li><li><strong>Joint Mediation rule:</strong> Core states ought to negotiate to end fault line conflicts as much as possible.</li><li><strong>Commonalities rule:</strong> All civilizations ought to search for common values, institutions, and practices and emphasize them in international dealings with each other.</li></ol><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Politics (book)\">Politics (book)</a><ul><li>Another book that regards the study of politics.</li></ul></li></ul>","The committee system in Congress enables specialization and increases efficiency":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The committee system in Congress enables specialization, since individual congress people are able to work in the area that they are most familiar with. At the same time, it increases efficiency since the people most familiar with a topic are able to work on that topic with a small group of other people proficient in the topic, and many of these groups can work at the same time.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains the benefits of a key institution in Congress.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself\">Congress is the only branch of government that has checks on itself</a><ul><li>A related characteristic of Congress.</li></ul></li></ul>","The elimination of salvation through the Church and the sacraments separates Calvinism from Catholicism":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The starkest disparity between Calvinism and Catholicism is Calvinism’s rejection of salvation through the Church, .</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This feeds into Weber’s thesis regarding the capitalist spirit, as the idea of a divine calling, to which he attributes the capitalist spirit, is individualized, and thus, cannot appear in a Catholic setting.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian\">The idea of a divine calling is uniquely Judeo-Christian</a><ul><li>Removing sacramental salvation is what forces the calling to be worked out in worldly labour instead.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South\">The capitalist spirit developed in the religious North of the US, rather than the commercial South</a><ul><li>The geographic evidence for this doctrinal difference producing economic consequences.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling\">The spirit of capitalism was enhanced by the idea of a divine calling</a></li></ul>","The erosion of Christianity is a large factor in the decline of the West":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The erosion of Christianity means that the cultural foundations of many of the threads that holds the West together flay and thus crumble. Many of the institutions built upon Christian foundations are left without their original foundations, which weakens them.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This explains a factor in the weakening of the West.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion\">Christian morality is correct because it has survived even beyond the bounds of the religion</a><ul><li>Even if this is correct, there are other reasons that Christianity should be kept around.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"A post-Christian culture is not sustainable\">A post-Christian culture is not sustainable</a><ul><li>A closely-related idea.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism\">The US should embrace Western culture and reject multiculturalism</a><ul><li>Related idea.</li></ul></li></ul>","The imprecision of moral intuitions is the reason for most moral conflicts":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>It seems that most of the time, moral conflicts arise because of a conflict between different individual’s moral intuitions, specifically when it comes to weighing different factors of moral importance.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This seems to suggest that we need some moral system beyond merely intuition, since our intuitions are extremely unpredictable and indeterministic.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>Human moral intuitions, even within the individual, can often conflict with each other. This seems to suggest that perhaps some moral intuitions are innate, while others are imposed upon us by outside influences, and therein lies the conflict.</p><ul><li>Perhaps it is like this: we (all sane people) each have same very similar set of fundamental moral axioms, upon which we do our own moral deduction and thus produce our own set of moral principles. However, this deduction has multiple equilibria, and our milieu includes people who arrived at different moral solutions, which, in our education, is then imposed upon us. The result is that each of us grow up with different sets of moral equilibria which are superimposed over each other.</li></ul><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"One must discard knowledge\">One must discard knowledge</a><ul><li>Perhaps the correct strategy then is really a Taoist/Neo-Confucianist one; that is, to discard moral knowledge that is not genuinely one’s own.</li></ul></li></ul>","Threats to economic freedom come from guilds, corporations and local bodies as much as from the central state":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>The classical worry about interference with economic activity was directed at parish councils, church wardens, guilds and chartered companies, all of which were part of eighteenth-century government, and not at a central bureaucracy of the modern kind.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Reading that worry as an argument against the national state specifically imports a distinction between public and private power that the original argument does not make.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>A contemporary instance of private restriction functioning as regulation.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society\">Licensure is, under most circumstances, unnecessary and detrimental to society</a><ul><li>The guild structure in its modern form.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents\">International IPR and patent laws benefit incumbents</a><ul><li>The same pattern at the level of international rule-making.</li></ul></li></ul>","We should not have more friends than we can get intimate with":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>Aristotle argues that the perfect number of friends allows us to get intimate with each friend.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>Aristotle sets a rule for the number of friends we ought to have, because otherwise, since it seems that having friends is good, we ought to have as many as possible.</p><h4>Addendum</h4><p>If we have more friends than we could get intimate with, it would be difficult to sympathize or get devoted to the friend.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"The virtuous man needs friends\">The virtuous man needs friends</a><ul><li>This is the principle that expands upon the concept that the virtuous man needs friends.</li></ul></li></ul>","When there is innovation, incumbents in a market are at a disadvantage":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>When considering the case of innovation, being an incumbent in a market may actually be a disadvantage.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This turns common antitrust logic on its head.</p><h4>Explanation</h4><p>When there is a new technology being introduced that has the capability to replace an existing technology, having large sunk costs can actually be a barrier to change rather than a barrier to entry. For instance, the new model of LLM search has the capability to replace web search. This means that if Google were to invest in LLM search, they would essentially be cannibalizing a crucial part of their current services in order to engage with this new innovation. On the other hand, established players in a field, by innovating, reduce the market share of the incumbent, so it benefits them to innovate.</p><h4>References</h4><ul><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries\">IBM failed to invest in emerging technologies, leading to the declining market share in many industries</a><ul><li>A concrete case of the incumbent disadvantage.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs\">Japanese companies are fundamentally structured to favor incremental progress over drastic breakthroughs</a><ul><li>Suggests the disadvantage can be structural, not merely a matter of incumbent complacency.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation\">Interlocking patents is a barrier to innovation</a><ul><li>Having interlocking patents then, would be a way for incumbents in a market to retain their dominance in the market.</li></ul></li><li><a class=\"wl\" data-note=\"Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation\">Most industries do not require patents to generate new knowledge; however, preexisting patents slow innovation</a><ul><li>Same as above.</li></ul></li></ul>","While nuclear weapons served to unite the world under a common order in the past, today, it serves to fracture the global system":"<h4>Summary</h4><p>While there was a nuclear monopoly/oligopoly in the world, the leading powers were able to use the influence of said nukes to ensure stability in the global system. However, with nuclear proliferation, the global system is likely to fragment.</p><h4>Significance</h4><p>This means that nuclear proliferation would be something that the current holders of nukes desperately do not want to occur.</p><h4>Examples</h4><ul><li>China helped Iran and Pakistan develop nukes. Part of the motivation for this would be to challenge the dominance of the US</li><li>Today, there is not a race to build more/better nukes. Rather, it is race to prevent nations that do not yet have nukes from building any.</li></ul><h4>References</h4>"});
